{
  "findings": [
    {
      "title": "Primary endpoint inconsistency: Section 2.1.2 states PFS is based on independent radiologic assessment, but Section 3 states BICR; also Section 2.1.2 says 'independent radiologic assessment' while Section 3 says 'blinded independent central review' - these are consistent, but the primary endpoint definition in Section 2.1.2 does not mention censoring at new anti-cancer therapy, while the estimand in Section 3 does; this is a discrepancy in the definition of PFS.",
      "page": 38,
      "quote": "Progression will be based on an independent radiologic assessment of disease response per Response Evaluation Criteria in Solid Tumors version 1.1 (RECIST v1.1).",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent KRAS mutation nomenclature: Section 2.2.1.2 uses 'KRAS p.G12C' but Section 2.2.2 uses 'KRAS G12C' and 'KRAS pG12C' (page 37) - inconsistent notation.",
      "page": 37,
      "quote": "AMG 510 offers an alternative treatment option, with a favorable benefit-risk profile, for patients with KRAS pG12C mutations.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement about ECOG performance status: Section 2.1.1 says patients with ECOG status ≥2 are not eligible for docetaxel and would receive palliative care, but Section 2.1.1 also says subjects enrolled must have ECOG status ≤1, which is consistent, but the text on page 37 says 'Patients who are not eligible for treatment with docetaxel (eg, patients with ECOG status ≥ 2) would most likely receive only palliative care' - this is not a contradiction but the protocol does not specify the inclusion criterion for ECOG in the excerpt; however, the statement 'subjects enrolled in this study must have better performance status (ECOG status ≤ 1)' on page 38 is consistent.",
      "page": 38,
      "quote": "Compared with the overall population of patients with NSCLC, the subjects enrolled in this study must have better performance status (ECOG status ≤ 1).",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent reporting of adverse events: On page 44, it says 'Thirty-five subjects (36.8%) had adverse events that were grade ≥ 3 in severity; anemia (5 subjects [5.3%]), diarrhea (4 subjects [4.2%]), metastatic CRC, increased alanine aminotransferase (ALT) (3 subjects [3.2%] each), and dyspnea, metastatic lung cancer, adult failure to thrive, respiratory failure, and vomiting (2 subjects [2.1%] each) were reported in > 1 subject' - but 'metastatic CRC' is listed as a grade ≥3 adverse event, which is a disease progression event, not an adverse event; this is a factual error.",
      "page": 44,
      "quote": "metastatic CRC, increased alanine aminotransferase (ALT) (3 subjects [3.2%] each)",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent number of subjects with treatment-related adverse events: On page 45, it says 'Treatment-related (as reported by the investigator) treatment-emergent adverse events were reported for 43 subjects (45.3%).' But the sum of subjects with specific treatment-related AEs listed (diarrhea 16, nausea 8, increased ALT 6, vomiting 5, increased ALP 4, fatigue 4, abdominal pain 3, dry mouth 3, anemia 3, increased CPK 2, decreased WBC 2, decreased appetite 2, peripheral neuropathy 2, pruritus 2) exceeds 43, but that is possible because subjects can have multiple AEs; however, the text says 'increased ALT (6 subjects [6.3%] each)' - the 'each' is misplaced, and the percentage 6.3% for 6 subjects out of 95 is 6.3%, but the text says 'increased ALT (6 subjects [6.3%] each)' which is grammatically incorrect and may imply that each of the listed events has 6 subjects, but only increased ALT has 6; the others have different numbers.",
      "page": 45,
      "quote": "increased ALT (6 subjects [6.3%] each), vomiting (5 subjects [5.3%]), increased blood alkaline phosphatase (ALP), fatigue (4 subjects [4.2%] each)",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement about docetaxel dose in Japan: On page 37, it says 'the standard dosing regimen in Japan is 60 mg/m2' but the protocol does not specify whether the study will use 60 mg/m2 for Japanese subjects or 75 mg/m2 for all; this is a potential inconsistency in the comparator dosing.",
      "page": 37,
      "quote": "the standard dosing regimen in Japan is 60 mg/m2 based on the occurrence of myelosuppression at higher doses (Taguchi et al, 1994).",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Missing information: The protocol excerpt does not include the study design, sample size, randomization ratio, or treatment arms, which are essential for a clinical trial protocol.",
      "page": 37,
      "quote": "Study 20190009, docetaxel has been chosen as the active control treatment",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent definition of ORR: In Section 2.1.3, ORR is defined as 'complete response (CR) + PR' but the endpoint table in Section 3 says 'response [PR]' and 'Complete response and PR require confirmatory repeat radiologic assessment' - the definition is incomplete and inconsistent.",
      "page": 39,
      "quote": "Objective response rate is defined as complete response (CR) + PR, as assessed by RECIST v1.1.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent survival rate: On page 39, it says 'The 5-year survival rate for advanced NSCLC cancer is between 6% and 33% depending on the stage' and on page 39 again it repeats the same, but on page 40 it says 'The 5-year survival rate for advanced NSCLC cancer is between 6% and 33% depending on the stage' - this is consistent, but the range is very wide and may be incorrect; however, it is not a contradiction within the excerpt.",
      "page": 39,
      "quote": "The 5-year survival rate for advanced NSCLC cancer is between 6% and 33% depending on the stage",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent reporting of AMG 510 clinical data: On page 44, it says 'As of the 12 June 2019 data cutoff date, Study 20170543 had enrolled 62 subjects, 27 with NSCLC, 31 with CRC, and 4 with other solid tumors.' But on page 47, it repeats the same numbers, which is consistent, but the text on page 44 says 'Thirty-nine of the 62 enrolled subjects were evaluable for efficacy' and then '7 of 17 evaluable subjects having a PR' - the denominator 17 is not explained; it is inconsistent with the 39 evaluable subjects.",
      "page": 44,
      "quote": "7 of 17 evaluable subjects having a PR (5 confirmed) and 8 subjects having stable disease (2 subjects had progression).",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement about KRAS mutation frequency: On page 40, it says 'approximately 80% occur at codon 12' and 'The KRAS p.G12C mutation is estimated to occur in approximately 13% of lung adenocarcinoma' - but on page 41 it says 'the incidence of G12C KRAS mutation in these tumor types is relatively low (approximately 13%)' - this is consistent, but the text on page 41 says 'the overall incidence of NSCLC and the poor prognosis of subjects with advanced/metastatic disease makes this mutation an important molecular target' - no contradiction.",
      "page": 40,
      "quote": "approximately 80% occur at codon 12",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent use of 'KRAS p.G12C' vs 'KRAS G12C' in the same section: On page 41, it says 'AMG 510 is a small molecule that specifically and irreversibly inhibits the KRAS G12C mutated protein' and later 'KRAS G12C' but the mutation is p.G12C; this is a nomenclature inconsistency.",
      "page": 41,
      "quote": "AMG 510 is a small molecule that specifically and irreversibly inhibits the KRAS G12C mutated protein.",
      "severity": "minor"
    }
  ]
}
